**Reasoning:**

1. **Multi-source grafting (main gain):** The single-source variant forces every new destination's grafting path to start at `src`, even when a much cheaper attachment point exists at an intermediate tree node. Multi-source Dijkstra over all tree nodes (with used edges zeroed) is the classical Takahashi–Matsuyama Steiner heuristic and typically yields noticeably cheaper trees. The previous failure was purely a path-reconstruction bug — `node_path` now stores the full path from `src` to every tree node, so `node_path[p[0]] + p[1:]` always yields a contiguous, valid `src→dst` path.

2. **More diverse candidates:** Adding multi-source variants of every existing seed strategy (plain, farthest-first, per-destination seeds) doubles the candidate pool at modest runtime cost (graphs are small region-level topologies), increasing the chance one variant escapes a bad greedy ordering.

3. **Local improvement pass:** After selecting the best tree, each destination is re-routed via shortest path on the residual graph where shared edges cost 0; any strictly cheaper edge set is kept. This catches suboptimal grafts the greedy left behind. The loop terminates because total cost strictly decreases each iteration.

All paths remain simple, start at `src`, and use only real directed edges, so validation (partition population + path continuity) will pass.
